        </div>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink leading-tight mb-4">
          What to Do After a Car Accident — Complete Step-by-Step Guide 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mb-2">By <strong>FreeInjuryCalc Editorial Team</strong> &bull; Reviewed by <strong>Sarah Jenkins, Esq.</strong></p>
      </header>

      <nav class="p-5 border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft rounded-lg mb-10 text-xs">
        <p class="font-bold text-ink mb-3 font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-[10px]">Table of Contents</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5 text-link">
          <li><a href="#scene" class="hover:underline">At the Scene — Immediate Steps</a></li>
          <li><a href="#medical" class="hover:underline">Medical Care — The Most Important Step</a></li>
          <li><a href="#insurance" class="hover:underline">Reporting to Insurance</a></li>
          <li><a href="#documentation" class="hover:underline">Building Your Documentation File</a></li>
          <li><a href="#mistakes" class="hover:underline">Critical Mistakes That Destroy Claims</a></li>
          <li><a href="#attorney" class="hover:underline">When to Call an Attorney</a></li>
          <li><a href="#settlement" class="hover:underline">Starting Your Settlement Claim</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <article class="prose dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-xs sm:text-sm text-body leading-relaxed space-y-6">
        <p>The minutes and hours after a car accident are critical. What you do — and what you fail to do — directly affects your safety, your legal rights, and the value of any insurance claim you later make. Most people are shaken, confused, and unsure where to start. This guide gives you a clear, step-by-step checklist so you know exactly what to do.</p>

        <h2 id="scene" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: At the Scene — Immediate Actions</h2>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Check for Injuries and Call 911</h3>
        <p>Your first priority is safety — not your phone, not your car, and not the other driver's insurance information. Check yourself and your passengers for injuries. Do not move anyone who may have a neck or back injury. Call 911 immediately if anyone is injured, if vehicles are blocking traffic, or if the other driver appears intoxicated. In most states, you are legally required to call police for accidents involving injury or significant property damage.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Move to Safety if Possible</h3>
        <p>If the vehicles are drivable and it is safe to do so, move them out of traffic to the shoulder or a nearby parking lot. Turn on your hazard lights. If the car cannot be moved, get all occupants to a safe location away from traffic before anything else.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Exchange Information — But Don't Overshare</h3>
        <p>Exchange the following with the other driver:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-4 space-y-1">
          <li>Full name and contact information</li>
          <li>Driver's license number and state</li>
          <li>Vehicle registration (make, model, year, license plate)</li>
          <li>Insurance company name and policy number</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Do <strong>not</strong> apologize, admit fault, or speculate about what happened — even a casual "I'm so sorry" can be used as an admission of liability. Stick to exchanging factual information only.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Document the Scene Thoroughly</h3>
        <p>Before anyone moves the vehicles (or immediately after moving to safety), photograph and video everything:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-4 space-y-1">
          <li>All vehicles from multiple angles — close-up and wide shots</li>
          <li>Damage to every vehicle involved</li>
          <li>The road, including skid marks, debris, traffic signs, and traffic lights</li>
          <li>The final resting position of the vehicles</li>
          <li>Any visible injuries (bruising, cuts, swelling)</li>
          <li>Weather and road conditions</li>
          <li>The other driver's license plate and insurance card (photograph both)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Talk to Witnesses</h3>
        <p>If bystanders saw the accident, approach them before the scene disperses and ask if they are willing to give their name and phone number. Witness accounts from uninvolved parties are highly credible evidence — they carry far more weight than the conflicting accounts of the drivers involved.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Get the Police Report Information</h3>
        <p>When officers arrive, cooperate fully with their investigation. Get the officer's name, badge number, and the incident report number. You can usually obtain the full accident report from your local police department or state DMV within 5 to 10 business days — this document becomes a cornerstone of your insurance claim.</p>

        <h2 id="medical" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Medical Care — The Single Most Important Post-Accident Step</h2>
        <p>Seek medical evaluation <strong>within 24 to 72 hours</strong> of the accident — even if you feel okay. This is the most important thing you can do for both your health and your legal claim. Here is why:</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Delayed Injury Onset Is Common</h3>
        <p>Adrenaline masks pain. Whiplash, concussions, internal bleeding, and spinal injuries frequently do not produce obvious symptoms until 24 to 48 hours after impact. By the time you feel the full extent of your injuries, the adrenaline has worn off and the inflammation has fully developed. Waiting to see a doctor means your medical records will show a gap — which insurance adjusters use to argue your injuries were pre-existing or unrelated to the accident.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">The Medical Record Is Your Legal Evidence</h3>
        <p>Your doctor's chart notes are the primary evidence connecting your injuries to the accident. Every symptom you describe to your doctor gets recorded. If you do not tell your doctor about a symptom, it does not exist for insurance purposes. Be thorough — mention every symptom, no matter how minor it seems: headaches, neck stiffness, lower back pain, numbness, dizziness, sleep disruption, and mood changes.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Continue All Treatment Until MMI</h3>
        <p>Follow every treatment recommendation your doctor makes. Attend every physical therapy session, every specialist appointment, every follow-up. Never settle your claim before reaching Maximum Medical Improvement (MMI) — the point at which your condition has stabilized. Settling early means you cannot claim future medical costs.</p>

        <h2 id="insurance" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Reporting to Insurance — What to Say and What Not To</h2>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Report to Your Own Insurer Promptly</h3>
        <p>Call your own insurance company within 24 to 48 hours to report the accident. Most policies require "prompt" reporting. You can report the facts — date, time, location, vehicles involved — without speculating about fault or admitting injury severity at this early stage.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Do NOT Give a Recorded Statement to the Other Driver's Insurer</h3>
        <p>The other driver's insurance company will call you quickly — often within hours. They will ask for a "brief recorded statement." You are not legally required to give one. The adjuster's goal is to get you to minimize your injuries, make statements inconsistent with your medical records, or unknowingly admit partial fault. Politely decline any recorded statement until you have consulted with an attorney.</p>

        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mt-6 mb-2">Do Not Accept Any Settlement Offer Immediately</h3>
        <p>Insurance companies sometimes offer small, quick settlements within days of an accident — before you have finished treatment or know the full extent of your injuries. Never accept any offer before reaching MMI. These early offers are almost always far below the claim's true value.</p>

        <h2 id="documentation" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Building Your Documentation File</h2>
        <p>From day one, maintain an organized file — physical or digital — containing everything related to your accident and recovery:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Pain journal:</strong> A dated daily log of your pain levels (0-10), specific symptoms, activities you could not do, sleep quality, and emotional state. This is powerful evidence for your pain and suffering claim.</li>
          <li><strong>All medical bills:</strong> Every invoice from every provider — keep originals and copies.</li>
          <li><strong>Medical records:</strong> Request copies of all visit notes, imaging reports (MRI, X-ray), and specialist opinions. You have a legal right to these.</li>
          <li><strong>Wage loss documentation:</strong> Letter from your employer on company letterhead confirming your salary, dates missed, and total lost income.</li>
          <li><strong>Repair estimates and receipts:</strong> All vehicle repair costs, rental car expenses, rideshare costs incurred while without your vehicle.</li>
          <li><strong>Out-of-pocket expenses:</strong> Prescription costs, over-the-counter medications, heating pads, braces, parking at medical facilities — keep every receipt.</li>
          <li><strong>All correspondence:</strong> Every letter, email, and text from insurance companies. Note the date, time, and content of every phone call.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="mistakes" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Critical Mistakes That Destroy Claims</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Apologizing at the scene</strong> — even "I'm sorry" can be used as a fault admission.</li>
          <li><strong>Waiting to see a doctor</strong> — every day of delay hurts your causation argument.</li>
          <li><strong>Posting on social media</strong> — investigators monitor every platform. Lock everything down.</li>
          <li><strong>Giving a recorded statement without counsel</strong> — almost always damages your claim.</li>
          <li><strong>Settling before MMI</strong> — you cannot recover future medical costs after signing a release.</li>
          <li><strong>Missing the statute of limitations</strong> — check your state's deadline in our <a href="/blog/how-long-after-car-accident-can-you-claim/" class="text-link hover:underline">claims deadline guide</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Gaps in medical treatment</strong> — adjusters argue that gaps mean you were not in that much pain.</li>
          <li><strong>Accepting the first offer</strong> — first offers are almost always 30% to 50% below fair value.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="attorney" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: When to Call an Attorney</h2>
        <p>Consider contacting a personal injury attorney if:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2">
          <li>You have any significant injuries requiring ongoing treatment</li>
          <li>Liability is disputed or unclear</li>
          <li>A commercial vehicle or government vehicle was involved</li>
          <li>The other driver was uninsured or underinsured</li>
          <li>The insurance company is acting in bad faith or lowballing aggressively</li>
          <li>You are approaching your state's statute of limitations without a fair settlement</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Most personal injury consultations are free. For a full cost-benefit analysis of attorney representation, see our guide: <a href="/blog/do-i-need-a-lawyer-for-car-accident/" class="text-link hover:underline">Do I Need a Lawyer for a Car Accident?</a></p>

        <h2 id="settlement" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Step 7: Starting Your Settlement Claim</h2>
        <p>Once you reach MMI, you are ready to pursue your settlement. The process involves:</p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mb-4 space-y-2">
          <li>Calculate your total damages using our <a href="/" class="text-link hover:underline font-semibold">free settlement calculator</a></li>
          <li>Compile all documentation into a demand package</li>
          <li>Send a formal demand letter to the at-fault driver's insurer</li>
          <li>Negotiate through the offer-and-counter process</li>
          <li>Accept a fair settlement or escalate to litigation</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Read our complete negotiation guide: <a href="/blog/how-to-negotiate-car-accident-settlement/" class="text-link hover:underline">How to Negotiate a Car Accident Settlement</a>. And understand the full formula in: <a href="/blog/how-car-accident-settlements-are-calculated/" class="text-link hover:underline">How Car Accident Settlements Are Calculated</a>.</p>

        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3 my-8">
          <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 bg-link text-white font-semibold text-xs px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg hover:opacity-90 transition-opacity duration-150">Calculate My Settlement Value →</a>
          <a href="/blog/how-to-negotiate-car-accident-settlement/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 border border-hairline text-link text-xs font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg hover:bg-canvas-soft transition-colors duration-150">Negotiation Guide</a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="text-lg font-bold text-ink mt-8 mb-4">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Should I call my insurance even if the accident wasn't my fault?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">Yes — always report the accident to your own insurer promptly, regardless of fault. Most policies contractually require notification of any accident. Your own insurer may need to activate your collision coverage for vehicle repairs while the liability claim is pending, handle a MedPay or PIP claim for your medical bills, or provide Uninsured/Underinsured Motorist coverage if the at-fault driver lacks adequate insurance. Failing to notify your own insurer can void coverage that you may need.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              What if the other driver has no insurance?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">If the other driver is uninsured, your options depend on your own coverage. If you carry Uninsured Motorist (UM) coverage — which is required in many states and highly recommended in all — you can file a claim with your own insurer under that coverage for your injuries and sometimes property damage. If you do not have UM coverage, you may need to sue the at-fault driver personally, though collecting a judgment from someone with no assets or insurance is often difficult. Always carry UM/UIM coverage.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              How long do I have to file a claim after a car accident?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">For insurance claims, report promptly per your policy terms — typically within days to weeks. For lawsuits, the statute of limitations varies by state, typically 1 to 3 years from the date of the accident. Some states give as little as 1 year (Kentucky, Louisiana, Tennessee) and some as many as 6 years (Maine, North Dakota). Government vehicle accidents often require formal notice within just 30 to 180 days. See our full <a href="/blog/how-long-after-car-accident-can-you-claim/" class="text-link hover:underline">state-by-state deadlines guide</a>.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              What if I was partially at fault for the accident?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">In most states, shared fault reduces — but does not eliminate — your recovery. Under "pure comparative negligence" (California, New York, Florida, etc.), you can recover even if you are 99% at fault, though your recovery is reduced by your percentage of fault. Under "modified comparative negligence" (most states), you can recover as long as you are less than 50% or 51% at fault. Only a few states use "contributory negligence" (Maryland, Virginia, Alabama, North Carolina, D.C.), where any fault on your part can entirely bar recovery. See our guide on <a href="/blog/how-car-accident-settlements-are-calculated/" class="text-link hover:underline">how fault affects your settlement</a>.</p>
          </details>
          <details class="group p-4 border border-hairline bg-canvas rounded-lg transition-all duration-200">
            <summary class="flex justify-between items-center font-semibold text-xs text-ink cursor-pointer list-none">
              Can I handle the insurance claim myself without a lawyer?
              <span class="transition group-open:rotate-180 text-mute"><svg class="w-4 h-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" /></svg></span>
            </summary>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mt-3">Yes — for minor accidents with clear liability and modest injuries, self-representation is reasonable and practical. Use our <a href="/" class="text-link hover:underline">free calculator</a> to know your claim's value before negotiating. For moderate to serious injuries, an attorney almost always produces significantly higher net recovery even after their contingency fee. Read our honest breakdown: <a href="/blog/do-i-need-a-lawyer-for-car-accident/" class="text-link hover:underline">Do I Need a Lawyer?</a></p>
          </details>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-10 p-5 border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft-2 rounded-lg text-[10px] text-mute leading-relaxed">
          <p class="font-bold text-ink mb-1 text-xs">Disclaimer</p>
          <p>The information provided in this article is for general informational purposes only and does not constitute legal advice. Laws vary by state. Consult a licensed personal injury attorney in your state for advice specific to your situation. FreeInjuryCalc.com is not a law firm and does not provide legal services.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

